; ======================================================================
;  THE LINK SEAM'S BOARD HALF - 2026-09-04, forum topic 591, the ruling of
;  2026-09-02 that net/ping/dns must ride whichever link is up.
; ======================================================================
;  RaspberryPi4/Lib/link.pi4 holds the POLICY (which interface wins) and
;  the MECHANISM (put a frame on it, take a frame off it). What it
;  deliberately does not hold is the FACTS, because the facts live in
;  the settings store and in two drivers' state, and a library that read
;  the settings store would be a library that could not be gated.
;
;  HwLinkOpen() below is where the facts are gathered and handed to
;  LinkSelect, and it is the ONLY place in the monitor that decides
;  which interface a command will use. Every command calls it, through
;  the seam - it was called LinkBringUp until 2026-09-05, which read as
;  a portable name and hid the fact that the shared core was reaching
;  into a board file for it.
;
;  WHY IT DOES NOT ALWAYS GO LOOKING FOR A CABLE
;  ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Bringing the wired controller up costs #ETH_LINK_MS - five seconds -
;  waiting for a link and for auto-negotiation, and on this bench the
;  usual state is a board on Wi-Fi with a lease and NOTHING plugged into
;  the Ethernet socket. Spending five silent seconds looking for a cable
;  that was not there a minute ago, before every ping, while a working
;  radio waits, is not what "Ethernet preferred when both" asks for -
;  it is a monitor that feels broken.
;
;  So the wired side is brought up when it is ALREADY up (a live
;  GenetPhyLinkUp re-check, which is one MDIO read), when the radio has
;  nothing to offer, or when the operator has said `net link wired`.
;  Otherwise the radio carries it and `net` says why in a full sentence.
;  A cable plugged in mid-session is therefore picked up by `net link
;  wired`, or by the next command after the radio drops - and that
;  trade is written here rather than left to be discovered.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; THE OPERATOR'S PIN MOVED OUT ON 2026-09-05. It was #LINK_PREF_AUTO /
; _WIRED / _WIFI and a gLinkPref here, which put half of one decision in
; a board file and gave the seam a second vocabulary for a thing the
; #HW_LINK_* kinds already name. It is now LinkPin() in
; RaspberryPi4/Lib/link.pi4, holding a kind or #HW_LINK_NONE for "choose
; each time", and this file reads it rather than owning it.

; EthDown - stop the MAC. Safe to call when it was never up.
;
; THIS NAME STAYS, and it stays because two board files outside the
; network commands call it for one reason that has nothing to do with
; networking: RaspberryPi4/Board/cache.pi4 and
; RaspberryPi4/Board/hw_boot.pi4 stop the receive DMA before control
; leaves the monitor, so a running payload is never sharing memory with a
; live bus master. HwLinkClose() below is the seam's name for the same
; act and is what Anvil/Core/net_cmd.pbi calls; renaming this one would
; have gained nothing and moved a guarantee two other files depend on.
Procedure EthDown()
  ; gEthUp is the monitor's logical state, not a hardware interlock. A
  ; half-failed start can have enabled receive DMA before that flag was set,
  ; so always ask the idempotent driver stop to clear both enable paths.
  GenetStop()
  gEthUp = 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The returning-payload hardware lease.
;
;  A payload may use the CPU, caches and explicitly handed-over devices;
;  it does not own Anvil's image/BSS or unrelated devices. No recovery can
;  make arbitrary corruption of the monitor's RAM meaningful again, and
;  none is claimed here. GENET is different: its receive DMA is a live bus
;  master before the jump, so Anvil explicitly disables it and cannot enter
;  the payload unless the enable bits read back clear after a system barrier.
;  That is the strongest boundary this driver exposes: it proves the stop
;  writes reached the Device register block and were accepted there. It is
;  NOT a hardware idle/empty acknowledgement and cannot rule out a final
;  already-issued write. The RX arena is therefore still monitor-owned and
;  must not be repurposed by a returning payload.
;
;  The return value preserves only whether the wired controller was active.
;  IP addresses, provenance, lease timers, aliases, UDP listeners and the
;  direct-cable DHCP server already live in their owning software rows. They
;  are neither copied nor cleared. EthPayloadResume performs a complete,
;  bounded hardware start because the payload had the machine and the old
;  peripheral register contents cannot be trusted.
;
;  RESULTS FROM EthPayloadQuiesce:
;    -1  the stop command/readback boundary failed; DO NOT JUMP
;     0  it was not active; nothing is owed on return
;     1  it was active and must be restarted on return
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; eth_HwUp has four results rather than a Boolean because a failed start has
; two materially different end states. Zero means the controller was checked
; down and the prompt may continue. A negative result means the stop command or
; one of its three enable-field readbacks failed; treating that value as merely
; "not up" would let the monitor keep running beside a possibly live bus master.
#ETH_HWUP_UNSAFE  = -1
#ETH_HWUP_DOWN    = 0
#ETH_HWUP_STARTED = 1
#ETH_HWUP_ALREADY = 2

Procedure.i EthPayloadStopChecked()
  Define cmd.i
  Define tdma.i
  Define rdma.i

  If GenetStop() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; Device accesses to GENET are ordered with one another by the MMU mapping,
  ; and this dsb sy/isb makes the whole stop sequence complete before either
  ; a payload instruction or a monitor DRAM reuse can follow it. Readback is
  ; deliberately limited to the three enable fields the driver owns. There is
  ; no cited GENETv5 "DMA idle" acknowledgement in this driver, so do not turn
  ; these clear bits into a stronger claim than they make.
  a64_barrier()
  cmd  = PeekL(#GENET_BASE + #GENET_UMAC_CMD) & $FFFFFFFF
  tdma = PeekL(#GENET_BASE + #GENET_TDMA_CTRL) & $FFFFFFFF
  rdma = PeekL(#GENET_BASE + #GENET_RDMA_CTRL) & $FFFFFFFF
  a64_barrier()
  If (cmd & (#GENET_CMD_TX_EN | #GENET_CMD_RX_EN)) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If (tdma & #GENET_DMA_EN) <> 0 Or (rdma & #GENET_DMA_EN) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i EthPayloadQuiesce()
  Define was.i
  was = 0
  If gEthUp <> 0 Or GenetStarted() <> 0
    was = 1
  EndIf
  If EthPayloadStopChecked() = 0
    gEthUp = 0
    PrintN("!! the Ethernet stop command or an enable-field readback failed, so")
    PrintN("   a stopped controller was not established and the payload was")
    PrintN("   NOT run. Running it with a bus master still writing into Anvil's")
    PrintN("   receive memory would give two programs ownership of the same RAM.")
    EthWhyGenet()
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  gEthUp = 0
  ProcedureReturn was
EndProcedure

Procedure.i EthPayloadReclaim()
  ; This is called IMMEDIATELY after CallAddr, with the monitor's caches still
  ; in their handoff state. A payload may have started or partly programmed
  ; GENET even when it was down before entry, so software flags are irrelevant.
  If EthPayloadStopChecked() = 0
    gEthUp = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  gEthUp = 0
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i EthPayloadResume(was.i)
  Define r.i
  ; EthPayloadReclaim already established the stop/readback boundary before
  ; cache, display, DHCP or console recovery. This procedure only restores the
  ; physical controller that was active before entry.
  If was = 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  PrintN("The wired controller was active before the payload. Reinitialising it")
  PrintN("from hardware now; the wait is bounded by the Ethernet link timeout.")
  r = eth_HwUp()
  If r < 0
    ; The raw start already named the failed cleanup. Do not reset here: RunAt
    ; owns the captured payload x0 and must print all sixteen digits first.
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If r = #ETH_HWUP_DOWN
    ; A zero from eth_HwUp means its checked stop succeeded. The controller's
    ; three owned enable fields read back clear, although that is deliberately
    ; not described as proof that every previously issued DMA write is idle.
    PrintN("!! the payload returned, but the wired controller did not come back.")
    PrintN("   Its saved network identity is retained; no inactive link is being")
    PrintN("   reported as active. Fix the link and type net up to try it again.")
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwLinkClose - the seam's teardown. 1 if something was actually
;  stopped, 0 if there was nothing to stop.
;
;  THE RETURN VALUE IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS IS NOT JUST EthDown. `net
;  down` tells the operator either "the controller has been stopped" or
;  "it was not up, so nothing changed", and those are different
;  sentences about different states. It used to read gEthUp - a Pi 4
;  global - from the shared core to decide which. Now the board answers.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwLinkClose()
  Define was.i
  was = gEthUp
  EthDown()
  If was = 1
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  EthUp - bring the Ethernet up, or say in full why it did not.
;
;  IDEMPOTENT AND CHEAP THE SECOND TIME. Once the port is up it stays
;  up, so a run of get commands pays the bring-up once. The re-check is
;  a live one rather than a remembered flag: GenetStarted() says the
;  driver believes it started and GenetPhyLinkUp() is one MDIO read that
;  says the cable is still in. A cable pulled between two commands is a
;  thing that happens on a bench and a monitor that keeps insisting the
;  network is up would be lying.
;
;  THE WAIT IS ANNOUNCED BEFORE IT IS TAKEN, and the line is drained
;  before the wait starts. Five seconds of an unresponsive prompt is
;  indistinguishable from a hung board - that is the exact complaint
;  that produced OutBreak() in this file - so the operator is told what
;  is being waited for and for how long, and sees it before the silence
;  rather than after it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  eth_HwUp - the controller-and-identity bring-up, with NO IP
;  configuration. The shared core of EthUp, EthUpIp and EthLinkUp.
;
;  RETURNS:
;   -1  bring-up failed and the stop command or its enable-field readback also
;       failed. The caller MUST take the local firmware-reset path;
;    2  already up and the link is still there - the caller need not
;       reconfigure, net.pi4's wired row still holds whatever IP it had;
;    1  freshly brought up - the wired row's MAC is revalidated in net.pi4;
;       its existing logical address, lease, alias and listeners are kept;
;    0  it did not come up, and the reason has been printed.
;
;  WHY THIS EXISTS. Until ping, dns and dhcp were added, this bring-up
;  was one inline body inside EthUp. Those three need the SAME controller
;  bring-up with different amounts of configuration on top: dhcp needs no
;  IP at all (it has none yet), ping and dns need three addresses and not
;  the TFTP server, get and put need all four. One shared core is the
;  reason the four paths cannot drift about how the GENET comes up.
;
;  IT CLEARS NO ADDRESS ROW. It used to call NetInit(), which reset the
;  whole stack, and was narrowed to NetClearIPv4(WIRED). That was still
;  hardware start erasing logical state: a returning payload or `net down`
;  followed by `net up` lost a live lease, a link-local primary, the
;  direct-cable alias and its listener. A controller reset invalidates its
;  registers and DMA rings, not the software identity above it. The MAC is
;  re-applied below and the wired ARP entries are flushed; the address row,
;  DHCP client/server machines and listeners keep their own lifecycle.
;
;  THE WIRED ARP ROWS ARE STILL FLUSHED deliberately: a freshly restarted
;  MAC means every entry learned through its old incarnation is suspect.
;  The radio's independently learned neighbours are not touched.
;
;  The wired row's MAC is set again immediately below. An ordinary caller
;  may still apply a new address afterward; a payload return keeps the one
;  that was already bound.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i eth_HwUp()
  Define startErr.i
  If gEthUp = 1
    If GenetStarted() = 1
      If GenetPhyLinkUp() = 1
        ProcedureReturn #ETH_HWUP_ALREADY
      EndIf
    EndIf
    PrintN("The Ethernet was up and is not any more - the link has gone away since")
    PrintN("the last command. Bringing it up again from the beginning.")
    gEthUp = 0
  EndIf

  ; EVERY REBUILD STARTS FROM ONE PROVED-DOWN HARDWARE STATE. This is after
  ; the genuinely-live shortcut above, so an already working controller pays
  ; nothing. Every other path may be a cold start, a stale software flag, a
  ; lost link with the old DMA generation still enabled, or a retry after an
  ; incomplete caller. GenetStop is probe-independent: it addresses the fixed
  ; BCM2711 GENET block directly. EthPayloadStopChecked then orders and reads
  ; back the MAC TX/RX and both DMA enable fields. Only after that boundary can
  ; a later Probe/MAC/RX-region refusal honestly return #ETH_HWUP_DOWN.
  gEthUp = 0
  If EthPayloadStopChecked() = 0
    PrintN("!! the Ethernet rebuild could not establish a stopped controller.")
    PrintN("   Its stop command or an enable-field readback failed. The caller")
    PrintN("   must reset rather than continue beside a possibly active MAC or DMA.")
    EthWhyGenet()
    ProcedureReturn #ETH_HWUP_UNSAFE
  EndIf

  EthMacFetch()

  If GenetProbe() = 0
    PrintN("!! the Ethernet controller did not identify itself, so nothing was")
    PrintN("   done. This is the MAC inside the processor and it is always")
    PrintN("   present, so a failure here is not a cable and not a switch - it is")
    PrintN("   this monitor reading the wrong address or a part that is not a")
    PrintN("   BCM2711.")
    EthWhyGenet()
    ProcedureReturn #ETH_HWUP_DOWN
  EndIf

  If GenetSetMac(@gEthMac[0]) = 0
    PrintN("!! the Ethernet controller refused this board's hardware address, so")
    PrintN("   nothing was done.")
    EthWhyGenet()
    ProcedureReturn #ETH_HWUP_DOWN
  EndIf
  If GenetSetRxRegion(#ETH_RX_BASE, #ETH_RX_BYTES) = 0
    PrintN("!! the Ethernet controller refused the receive region, so nothing was")
    PrintN("   done. That region is a constant in this monitor and not something")
    PrintN("   you typed, so this is a fault in Anvil rather than in what you did.")
    EthWhyGenet()
    ProcedureReturn #ETH_HWUP_DOWN
  EndIf

  Print("Bringing the Ethernet up. Waiting up to ")
  PrintDec(#ETH_LINK_MS)
  PrintN(" milliseconds for the link")
  PrintN("to come up and for auto-negotiation to finish, so this pauses.")
  UartDrain()

  If GenetStart(#ETH_LINK_MS) = 0
    ; STOP WHAT IT ALREADY STARTED, BEFORE SAYING WHY IT FAILED.
    ;
    ; GenetStart() runs the bring-up in the driver's order and the link
    ; wait is near the END of it: by the time it fails for want of a
    ; cable it has already reset the UniMAC, initialised 256 receive
    ; descriptors pointing into #ETH_RX_BASE and ENABLED THE DMA. It
    ; does not undo any of that, and it is not wrong not to - a
    ; half-started controller is exactly what somebody diagnosing this
    ; wants to be able to read registers out of.
    ;
    ; It is wrong to LEAVE it that way at a live prompt, though. The one
    ; thing this monitor promises about #ETH_RX_BASE is that no bus
    ; master is writing there while a payload runs, and "the network
    ; failed to come up" is precisely the state somebody then types
    ; `run` from. The checked stop and its three enable-field readbacks remove
    ; the question or return the distinct unsafe result below.
    startErr = GenetError()
    If EthPayloadStopChecked() = 0
      PrintN("!! the Ethernet start failed, and its cleanup stop command or an")
      PrintN("   enable-field readback also failed. The caller must reset rather")
      PrintN("   than continue beside a possibly active MAC or DMA engine.")
      EthWhyGenet()
      ProcedureReturn #ETH_HWUP_UNSAFE
    EndIf
    If startErr = #GENET_ERR_NO_LINK
      PrintN("!! there is no Ethernet link, so the network is not up and nothing")
      PrintN("   was done. Nothing is plugged into this board's Ethernet socket, or")
      PrintN("   the other end of the cable is not powered, or the cable is bad. Put")
      PrintN("   one in, wait a moment for the socket lights, and type the command")
      PrintN("   again. This monitor waited five seconds and then answered, rather")
      PrintN("   than sitting here looking like a hung board.")
      ProcedureReturn #ETH_HWUP_DOWN
    EndIf
    If startErr = #GENET_ERR_ANEG
      PrintN("!! the Ethernet link came up but auto-negotiation never finished, so")
      PrintN("   nothing was done. The speed and duplex a PHY reports before")
      PrintN("   negotiation completes are left over from last time and acting on")
      PrintN("   them would configure the controller wrongly. This is usually an")
      PrintN("   old switch or a fixed-speed port at the other end. The network is")
      PrintN("   not up and nothing was done.")
      ProcedureReturn #ETH_HWUP_DOWN
    EndIf
    If startErr = #GENET_ERR_HALF_DUPLEX
      PrintN("!! the link negotiated HALF duplex and this driver refuses to run on")
      PrintN("   one, so the network is not up and nothing was done. Half duplex")
      PrintN("   means collisions, and the controller's collision handling is not")
      PrintN("   implemented here - genet.pi4 refuses it rather than passing")
      PrintN("   traffic badly. Set the port at the other end to full duplex, or")
      PrintN("   use a different port.")
      ProcedureReturn #ETH_HWUP_DOWN
    EndIf
    PrintN("!! the Ethernet controller did not start, so the network is not up and")
    PrintN("   nothing was done.")
    EthWhyGenet()
    ProcedureReturn #ETH_HWUP_DOWN
  EndIf

  ; Initialise mutable defaults only on the first software attachment.
  ; A hardware restart must not silently replace a checksum policy or any
  ; listener state the running monitor deliberately selected.
  If NetMacSet(#HW_LINK_WIRED) = 0
    NetDefaults()
  EndIf
  ; The controller has new rings, so neighbours learned through its old
  ; incarnation are stale. Flush only this interface; the radio keeps its
  ; independently learned neighbours and its entire identity row.
  NetArpFlush(#HW_LINK_WIRED)
  If NetSetMac(#HW_LINK_WIRED, @gEthMac[0]) = 0
    ; This logical refusal happened after GenetStart enabled the MAC and DMA.
    ; Reclaim the hardware before printing it. NetError is owned by net.pi4
    ; and is not changed by this GENET cleanup.
    If EthPayloadStopChecked() = 0
      PrintN("!! the network stack refused the wired address, and the controller")
      PrintN("   cleanup stop command or an enable-field readback failed. The caller")
      PrintN("   must reset rather than continue beside a possibly active MAC or DMA.")
      EthWhyGenet()
      ProcedureReturn #ETH_HWUP_UNSAFE
    EndIf
    PrintN("!! the network stack refused this board's hardware address, so nothing")
    PrintN("   was done.")
    EthWhyNet()
    ProcedureReturn #ETH_HWUP_DOWN
  EndIf

  gEthUp = 1
  ProcedureReturn #ETH_HWUP_STARTED
EndProcedure

; Ordinary monitor callers use this wrapper, never the raw four-result value directly.
; If an attempted bring-up cannot prove the three owned enable fields clear,
; there is no safe local prompt to return to. The returning-payload path is the
; one exception: it must first print the payload's complete captured x0, so
; EthPayloadResume propagates the negative result to RunAt instead.
Procedure.i eth_HwUpLocal()
  Define r.i
  r = eth_HwUp()
  If r < 0
    PrintN("The monitor is resetting through firmware because GENET could not be")
    PrintN("proved disabled after a failed local start.")
    UartDrain()
    SafetyToFirmware()
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn r
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  EthCableIn - IS THERE A CABLE IN THE SOCKET, cheaply, without starting
;  anything. 1 yes, 0 no or cannot tell.
;
;  WHY IT IS NOT eth_HwUp(). Bringing the controller up costs
;  #ETH_LINK_MS - five seconds - waiting for the link and for
;  auto-negotiation, and the boot path must not spend that on a board
;  with nothing plugged in. This asks the PHY instead: the identity
;  register (one 32-bit read, no clock to enable and no reset to
;  release), then the MDIO scan for the PHY address if it has not been
;  found yet, then the link-status bit. Bounded at 640 ms in the worst
;  case - a full 32-address sweep of a board whose PHY is not answering -
;  and a few milliseconds in the ordinary one.
;
;  THE PHY ADDRESS IS SCANNED HERE RATHER THAN ASSUMED. GenetPhyLinkUp
;  reads whatever address the driver has settled on, and before
;  GenetStart has run that is address zero, which is not this board's
;  PHY. Reading the link bit out of an address nobody answers at would
;  be a plausible-looking "no cable" that no cable could ever change.
;
;  A NEGATIVE FROM GenetPhyLinkUp IS AN MDIO FAULT AND NOT A DOWN LINK,
;  and the two must not be conflated: callers of that procedure are told
;  to test for negative before testing for zero. Both answer 0 here,
;  because for this question "cannot tell" and "no" lead to the same
;  next step - but the distinction is not lost, it is printed by the
;  bring-up that follows if one is asked for.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i EthCableIn()
  Define v.i
  If GenetProbe() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If GenetPhyAddress() = 0 And GenetPhyId() = 0
    If GenetPhyScan() < 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  EndIf
  v = GenetPhyLinkUp()
  If v = 1
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwLinkStaticUp - PUT THE STORED ADDRESS ON THE WIRE, NOW. Returns the
;  #HW_LINK_* kind that ended up carrying it, or #HW_LINK_NONE.
;
;  WHAT IT IS FOR, and it is the whole of the 2026-09-07 evening. The
;  three stored addresses reached the IP layer only as a SIDE EFFECT of
;  the next command that happened to choose an interface, and the
;  automatic choice deliberately will not pay the five-second wired
;  bring-up while a radio is working (the reasoning is at the top of this
;  file and it is right). So a board with `net address 192.168.137.2`
;  typed into it, a cable in the socket and a radio joined to something
;  else went on carrying its traffic over the radio, and the console with
;  it - and the machine on the other end of the cable could ping the
;  board and not talk to it.
;
;  THIS IS THE EXPLICIT ACT, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS ALLOWED TO PAY THE
;  WAIT. `net address` is somebody saying what this board's wired address
;  is; the boot fallback is a board with no lease and a cable in it. Both
;  are moments where five seconds spent on the socket is exactly what was
;  asked for. Nothing in the automatic path changed, so `ping` on a
;  cable-less bench still costs nothing.
;
;  EVERY ARM IS BOUNDED AND IT CANNOT SPIN:
;
;    no stored address     two settings reads and a parse
;    no cable              EthCableIn - a register read and at worst one
;                          MDIO sweep, 640 ms in the pathological case
;    cable, link not up    ONE eth_HwUp, which is the announced
;                          five-second link wait and then an answer
;    already up            one MDIO read
;
;  IT ASKS THE CABLE BEFORE IT PAYS FOR THE LINK, in that order, so a
;  board with the addresses stored and nothing plugged in answers in
;  milliseconds - which is the ordinary state of a bench that runs on
;  Wi-Fi and has a wired address left over from a previous session.
;
;  THE PREFERENCE ORDER IS NOT RE-IMPLEMENTED HERE. Once the controller
;  is up, HwLinkOpen is what chooses - with the wired side now presenting
;  BOTH a carrier and an address, which is LinkSelect's outright win, and
;  with the operator's `net link wifi` pin still able to overrule it.
;  That is why this answers the kind that actually won rather than 1: a
;  caller that assumed "wired" would print a sentence about the cable
;  while the radio carried the traffic.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwLinkStaticUp()
  If EthHasIpConfig() = 0
    ProcedureReturn #HW_LINK_NONE
  EndIf
  If EthCableIn() = 0
    ProcedureReturn #HW_LINK_NONE
  EndIf
  If gEthUp <> 1 Or GenetStarted() <> 1 Or GenetPhyLinkUp() <> 1
    If eth_HwUpLocal() <= #ETH_HWUP_DOWN
      ProcedureReturn #HW_LINK_NONE
    EndIf
  EndIf
  If HwLinkOpen(1) = 0
    ProcedureReturn #HW_LINK_NONE
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn LinkKind()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  EthBootWired - THE BOOT POLICY'S WIRED HALF. Called before the
;  wireless auto-join, from RaspberryPi4/Board/boot.pi4.
;
;  WHAT IT IS FOR. On 2026-09-07 this board had a cable running straight
;  to the machine that wanted to talk to it, an address waiting for it at
;  the other end, and NO CONSOLE, because the console could only ride the
;  radio and the wired side took no address until somebody typed a
;  command - over the console that did not exist. A board that comes up
;  on a cable must be reachable on that cable with nothing typed at it,
;  the same way a board that comes up on a radio has been since
;  2026-09-06.
;
;  THE ORDER IS LEASE FIRST, STORED ADDRESS SECOND, and it is that way
;  round on purpose. A DHCP server is the only thing on a segment that
;  can say an address is free; a stored address is this board's memory of
;  a segment it may not be plugged into any more. Preferring the memory
;  would put a board that has been moved onto a network it does not
;  belong to, silently, and the collision would land on whoever holds
;  that address now. So the wire is asked first and the memory is the
;  fallback - which is also the case the bench actually has, because a
;  cable straight into a Windows laptop has no DHCP server on it at all.
;
;  IT IS BOUNDED, IT IS LOUD, AND IT NEVER SPINS. Rule for anything new
;  in a boot path: it must not be able to strand the board. The costs,
;  written out because a boot that pauses is indistinguishable from a
;  hung one unless the arithmetic is on the screen:
;
;    no cable                 a few milliseconds, and it says so
;    cable, a server answers  the five-second link wait plus the
;                             exchange, which is two round trips
;    cable, nobody answers    the five-second link wait, then FOUR
;                             DISCOVERs at three seconds each - twelve
;                             seconds - and then the stored address if
;                             there is one, which costs one more MDIO
;                             read because the link is already up
;
;  The REQUEST retries cost nothing here: they only run after an OFFER
;  has arrived, and if an OFFER arrived there is a server.
;
;  ONE dhcp AND NOT A LOOP OF THEM, and that is deliberate: the command
;  already retries four whole exchanges internally, which is more retry
;  than the wireless loop beside it does with three. Wrapping it in an
;  outer loop would multiply a bounded twelve seconds into a minute of a
;  silent-looking board for exactly the case - a cable into a machine
;  whose DHCP server is not running - where the answer is not going to
;  change.
;
;  IT SAYS WHICH OF THE THREE HAPPENED, in one line beginning `wired:`,
;  because the boot log is the only account anybody has of a board that
;  came up unreachable and "it printed some Ethernet lines" is not an
;  account. The three are exhaustive:
;
;      wired: lease from <server>
;      wired: saved address <a.b.c.d>, no DHCP answer
;      wired: no cable
;
;  GIVING UP IS NOT FAILING. The console arms itself on whichever
;  interface ends up holding an address, re-derived on every spin of the
;  prompt, so a lease that arrives later - the radio joins, or somebody
;  types dhcp - brings the console up with nobody asking for it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  EthServeDhcp - NOTHING ANSWERED ON THIS CABLE, SO THIS BOARD HANDS
;  OUT THE ADDRESSES.  The bench ruling of 2026-09-07, in the words it
;  was given in: "if the pi is getting that 169 address it changes over
;  to dhcp and divvy out an address."
;
;  IT IS CALLED ONLY WHERE THE DISCOVERS WENT UNANSWERED, which is the
;  measurement the whole server role rests on - see the header of
;  Anvil/Core/dhcpd.pbi for why the board has to earn the seat rather
;  than assume it.
;
;  THE ONE REFUSAL, AND IT IS NOT A TIDINESS CHECK: if the address this
;  board already holds on the cable is itself inside 192.168.137.0/24,
;  the server is NOT started. That is somebody's saved static address on
;  the very subnet the pool comes out of, so serving it would mean
;  handing a peer an address out of a range this board is also sitting
;  in with no idea which parts of it are free - a collision the board
;  would create itself and then not be able to explain.
;
;  IT PRINTS WHAT IT DID in the boot log, with the pool, because a
;  machine that suddenly has a 192.168.137.x address it did not ask for
;  is entitled to an explanation somewhere.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure EthServeDhcp()
  If (gEthIp & DhcpdMask()) = (DhcpdSelf() & DhcpdMask())
    PrintN("wired: this board is NOT handing out addresses on this cable - the")
    PrintN("   address it holds is already inside the block the pool comes out of,")
    PrintN("   so it cannot tell which of those addresses are free.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If DhcpdStart(#HW_LINK_WIRED) = 0
    PrintN("!! this board could not take the DHCP server role on the cable, so a")
    PrintN("   machine set to DHCP at the other end will fall back to a link-local")
    PrintN("   address of its own. That still reaches this board - the console is")
    PrintN("   listening on 169.254.x.y as well - so nothing is lost, but a lease")
    PrintN("   would have been steadier.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  PrintN("wired: nothing answered a DHCP discover on this cable, so THIS BOARD is")
  Print("   the DHCP server on it now: ")
  PutIp(DhcpdSelf())
  Print(" for itself, leases from ")
  PutIp(#DHCPD_POOL_LO)
  Print(" to ")
  PutIp(#DHCPD_POOL_HI)
  PrintN(",")
  PrintN("   one hour each. A machine at the other end set to DHCP - which is what")
  PrintN("   an Ethernet port is by default - gets a real address with nothing")
  PrintN("   changed on it. If a real DHCP server ever answers on this cable, this")
  PrintN("   board gives the role up at once and takes a lease like anybody else.")
EndProcedure

Procedure EthBootWired()
  Define k.i
  If EthCableIn() = 0
    PrintN("wired: no cable. Nothing is plugged into the Ethernet socket, so no")
    PrintN("address was asked for and nothing was started. This costs no time and")
    PrintN("is the ordinary answer on a bench that runs on Wi-Fi.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  PrintN("Ethernet: the cable is in, so an address is asked for by DHCP first -")
  PrintN("a server on this segment is the only thing that can say an address is")
  PrintN("free. If nothing answers, the stored address is used; and if there is")
  PrintN("no stored address either, this board picks one for itself out of")
  PrintN("169.254.0.0/16 and proves it is free (RFC 3927), which is what the")
  PrintN("machine at the other end of a bare cable has already done.")
  UartDrain()

  If CmdDhcp() <> 0
    Print("wired: lease from ")
    PutIp(DhcpServerId())
    PrintN(".")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; THE STORED ADDRESS SECOND, AND IT IS TESTED BEFORE LINK-LOCAL ON
  ; PURPOSE. Somebody typed it and ran `settings save`; that outranks
  ; anything this board invents for itself, whatever the invention's
  ; provenance. A board with a saved address never reaches the third
  ; step at all, which is what "a saved static address wins over
  ; link-local" means in one `If`.
  ;
  ; NOTE THE ORDER OF THE TWO TESTS BELOW. EthHasIpConfig() now answers
  ; YES for a link-local address as well (netcfg.pbi), because
  ; everything downstream has to treat one like any other address - so
  ; reading it here as "there is a saved address" would be wrong the
  ; second time round. It cannot be, at this point in the boot: nothing
  ; has taken a link-local address yet, gEthLlIp is still zero, and the
  ; only thing this can be answering about is the settings store.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  If EthHasIpConfig() <> 0
    k = NetStaticApply()
    If k <> #HW_LINK_NONE
      Print("wired: saved address ")
      PutIp(gEthIp)
      PrintN(", no DHCP answer.")
      EthServeDhcp()
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
    PrintN("!! no DHCP server answered and the stored address could not be put on")
    PrintN("   the wire either. Trying a link-local address instead.")
  EndIf

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ; AND IF NOTHING ELSE, AN ADDRESS OF THIS BOARD'S OWN.  2026-09-07.
  ;
  ; This is the case the bench actually has and the one the whole lane
  ; exists for: a cable running straight into a laptop, no server on it,
  ; nothing saved, and until tonight NO ADDRESS AND THEREFORE NO
  ; CONSOLE - a board wired to the machine trying to reach it with no
  ; way in. The laptop at the other end had already given itself
  ; a link-local address for exactly the same reason. This is the board doing
  ; the same thing, by the same document.
  ;
  ; BOUNDED: nine seconds if nobody objects, nineteen in the worst case,
  ; and it prints the arithmetic before it waits. See
  ; Anvil/Core/netll.pbi.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  If NetLinkLocalAcquire(#HW_LINK_WIRED) <> 0
    Print("wired: link-local ")
    PutIp(gEthIp)
    PrintN(" (no DHCP answer).")
    ; ----------------------------------------------------------------
    ; AND NOW THE BOARD BECOMES THE SERVER. This is the case the bench
    ; named: a cable with nothing on it that hands out addresses, and a
    ; laptop at the other end whose Ethernet port is a DHCP client that
    ; nobody has answered. The link-local address is kept as well - it
    ; is the interface's PRIMARY address, probed and defended, and it is
    ; the only address a peer that is not a DHCP client can reach - so
    ; both peers are served, which is the whole point of the pair.
    ; ----------------------------------------------------------------
    EthServeDhcp()
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  PrintN("!! the cable is in and this board has no address on it at all - no")
  PrintN("   lease, nothing stored, and no link-local address could be taken.")
  PrintN("   Nothing is broken and nothing was left half-started: the boot")
  PrintN("   carries on and the console arms on whichever interface does end up")
  PrintN("   holding an address. Typing dhcp, or net address, arms it by itself.")
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwLinkSayDetail - what THIS BOARD has to add about the link in use,
;  once the shared command has printed everything that is the same on
;  every board.
;
;  WHAT THE COMMAND LAYER ALREADY PRINTS, so that nothing is said twice:
;  which interface won and why (LinkSay), the negotiated speed and duplex
;  (LinkSpeed / LinkDuplex over the HwLink* getters), the hardware
;  address frames leave with (PutLinkMac over LinkMacPtr), and the four
;  configured addresses. This used to be eth_SayUp() and printed all of
;  that itself, in this file, for the wired link only.
;
;  WHAT IS LEFT IS TRUE OF THIS BOARD AND NOTHING ELSE:
;
;    THE RECEIVE REGION. Half a megabyte at a fixed address that the MAC
;    writes into by DMA. An operator about to type `run` is entitled to
;    know where a bus master is writing, and the reassurance that it is
;    outside both payload windows is a fact about this memory map.
;
;    THE MADE-UP HARDWARE ADDRESS. When the VideoCore would not report
;    this board's own address, a locally administered one is used
;    instead - and that will not match a DHCP reservation or a switch
;    port locked to the address printed on the board. It is a warning
;    and it must not be lost.
;
;  IT SAYS NOTHING AT ALL WHEN THE RADIO CARRIES THE TRAFFIC. Neither
;  fact is about the radio, and a receive region printed under a Wi-Fi
;  status line would be an invitation to believe the wrong thing.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwLinkSayDetail()
  If LinkKind() <> #HW_LINK_WIRED
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If gEthMacKnown = 1 And gEthMacFirmware = 0
    PrintN("  This board's hardware address is MADE UP, not its own. The firmware")
    PrintN("  would not tell us the real one, so a locally administered address is")
    PrintN("  being used instead. It works on an ordinary network and it will NOT")
    PrintN("  match a DHCP reservation or a switch port locked to the address")
    PrintN("  printed on the board.")
  EndIf
  Print("  Received frames land at ")
  PutAddr(#ETH_RX_BASE)
  Print(", ")
  PrintDec(#ETH_RX_BYTES)
  PrintN(" bytes of them, which is")
  PrintN("  outside both payload windows so nothing you load can be put there.")
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  eth_SayCount - one counter, as a sentence, and only when it is not
;  zero unless it is one of the two that are meant to be printed anyway.
;
;  WHY MOST OF THEM ARE HIDDEN AT ZERO. Sixteen lines of "0" is a wall a
;  reader skims, and the whole point of this command is that one non-zero
;  number should be impossible to miss. The two that always print are the
;  packet counts, because "nothing has been counted at all" and "nothing
;  has gone wrong" are different answers and a screen with neither would
;  be indistinguishable from a command that did not run.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure eth_SayCount(*what, v.i, always.i)
  If v < 0
    Print("  ")
    UartWriteStr(*what)
    PrintN(": the controller would not answer that read, so this number is")
    PrintN("  not known. That is a fault in this monitor, not in the network.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If v = 0 And always = 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  Print("  ")
  UartWriteStr(*what)
  Print(": ")
  PrintDec(v)
  PrintN(".")
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwLinkSayCounters - WHAT THE ETHERNET CONTROLLER ITSELF COUNTED.
;
;  THIS EXISTS BECAUSE OF ONE DAY WITH NO NUMBER IN IT. On 2026-09-08
;  this board came up on a cable straight into the bench laptop during a
;  display bring-up, and between 17:38:57 and 17:41:16 the laptop's
;  Realtek Ethernet card logged "reset by tx hang" twelve times, at 11 to
;  13 second intervals, before Windows stopped the device. Nothing was
;  run against that card from the PC, so the only thing on the far end of
;  the cable was this board. The explanation that fitted - this board
;  holding 802.3x PAUSE asserted through a stretch when nothing was
;  reading its receive ring - had an EXACT ANSWER SITTING IN A UNIMAC
;  COUNTER, and no command in this monitor would print it. The bench lost
;  its Ethernet for the day and the diagnosis stayed a hypothesis.
;
;  It is not a hypothesis now, and the first line below is why: with
;  genet.pi4 setting CMD_TX_PAUSE_IGNORE on every link the MAC cannot
;  emit a PAUSE frame, so "pause frames sent" reads zero, and a boot on a
;  cable that leaves it at zero is the proof that this board is not the
;  thing stopping anybody's card.
;
;  THE COUNTS ARE SINCE THE INTERFACE CAME UP, not since the board did.
;  The bring-up zeroes the whole statistics block, so "times this
;  interface has been brought up" is printed beside them: a zero counter
;  on an interface rebuilt four seconds ago says much less than a zero
;  counter on one that has been up for an hour.
;
;  IT READS AND PRINTS. Nothing here writes a register, nothing here
;  clears a counter, and it is safe to type twice in a row - which is
;  exactly how anybody would use it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwLinkSayCounters()
  If LinkKind() <> #HW_LINK_WIRED
    PrintN("The interface carrying traffic is not the wired one, and the counters")
    PrintN("this command prints belong to the Ethernet controller. Type net link")
    PrintN("wired to move onto the cable, then ask again.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  If GenetStarted() <> 1
    PrintN("The Ethernet controller has not been started, so it has counted")
    PrintN("nothing. Type net up to bring it up, then ask again. Nothing was read")
    PrintN("and nothing was changed.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf

  PrintN("What the Ethernet controller has counted since this interface came up.")
  PrintN("A counter that is zero is not printed, apart from the two packet counts,")
  PrintN("so anything you see below is a number worth reading.")
  PrintNl()

  ; THE FLOW-CONTROL PAIR FIRST, AND THE FIRST LINE IS THE ONE THAT
  ; MATTERS. It is printed whatever it says, because "zero" is the
  ; answer this board owes anybody whose network card it is sharing a
  ; cable with.
  Print("  Pause frames this board SENT: ")
  PrintDec(GenetMibTxPause())
  PrintN(".")
  If GenetMibTxPause() > 0
    PrintN("  !! THAT MUST BE ZERO. This board is telling the machine at the other")
    PrintN("     end of the cable to stop transmitting, and a machine whose transmit")
    PrintN("     queue is held shut long enough resets its own network card. It is")
    PrintN("     how the bench laptop's Ethernet was stopped on 2026-09-08. The MAC")
    PrintN("     is configured never to send one, so a number here means that")
    PrintN("     configuration did not land - see THE PAUSE PROMISE in genet.pi4.")
  EndIf
  eth_SayCount("Pause frames the other end sent us", GenetMibRxPause(), 0)
  If GenetMibRxPause() > 0
    PrintN("     That is the other end asking US to stop, which this board ignores")
    PrintN("     on purpose. It is not a fault here; it means the machine on the")
    PrintN("     other end of the cable is the one that is behind.")
  EndIf
  PrintNl()

  eth_SayCount("Good frames the controller received", GenetMibRxPackets(), 1)
  eth_SayCount("Good frames the controller sent", GenetMibTxPackets(), 1)
  PrintNl()

  ; THE ERROR COUNTERS. Silent when they are zero, which is what a
  ; healthy gigabit link into one switch looks like.
  eth_SayCount("Frames arriving with a bad checksum", GenetMibRxFcsErrors(), 0)
  eth_SayCount("Frames arriving misaligned", GenetMibRxAlignErrors(), 0)
  eth_SayCount("Frames arriving with an impossible length", GenetMibRxLengthErrors(), 0)
  eth_SayCount("Frames arriving oversize", GenetMibRxOversize(), 0)
  eth_SayCount("Jabber", GenetMibRxJabber(), 0)
  eth_SayCount("Frames sent with a bad checksum", GenetMibTxFcsErrors(), 0)
  eth_SayCount("Frames sent oversize", GenetMibTxOversize(), 0)
  eth_SayCount("Late collisions", GenetMibTxLateColl(), 0)
  eth_SayCount("Excessive collisions", GenetMibTxExcessColl(), 0)
  If GenetMibTxLateColl() > 0 Or GenetMibTxExcessColl() > 0
    PrintN("     A full-duplex link has no collisions at all, so a collision count")
    PrintN("     means the two ends disagree about the duplex. That produces a link")
    PrintN("     that works and is dreadful, rather than one that fails.")
  EndIf

  ; THE TWO OVERRUN COUNTS, WHICH ARE THE SAME EVENT ONE STAGE APART.
  eth_SayCount("Frames dropped because the receive buffer was full", GenetRbufOverflows(), 0)
  eth_SayCount("Frames dropped because the receive ring was full", GenetRxDiscards(), 0)
  If GenetRbufOverflows() > 0 Or GenetRxDiscards() > 0
    PrintN("     Dropping a frame when nothing has read the ring recently is the")
    PrintN("     ORDINARY and correct outcome for a monitor - it is what this board")
    PrintN("     does INSTEAD of telling the other end to stop. Every protocol above")
    PrintN("     it copes, and the same stack runs over the radio, which has no flow")
    PrintN("     control of any kind.")
  EndIf
  eth_SayCount("Receive buffer errors", GenetRbufErrors(), 0)
  eth_SayCount("Address filter errors", GenetMdfErrors(), 0)
  PrintNl()

  ; AND THE THREE SOFTWARE COUNTS THAT SAY WHAT THE NUMBERS ABOVE ARE
  ; ABOUT. Every bring-up zeroes the hardware block, so this one has to
  ; print even at 1.
  Print("  Times this interface has been brought up: ")
  PrintDec(GenetStartCount())
  PrintN(". Each one clears every")
  PrintN("  counter above, so they are about the most recent bring-up only.")
  eth_SayCount("Times auto-negotiation was restarted", GenetAnegRestarts(), 0)
  If GenetAnegRestarts() > 1
    PrintN("     More than one is worth explaining. Restarting negotiation takes the")
    PrintN("     link down for seconds, and a machine whose cable keeps going away")
    PrintN("     mid-transmit is the other way to make it reset its network card.")
  EndIf
  eth_SayCount("Times the link was seen to drop and come back", GenetLinkDrops(), 0)
  If GenetLinkDrops() > 0
    PrintN("     That count is a floor and not a total: the PHY latches one drop")
    PrintN("     until somebody reads it, so any number of drops between two looks")
    PrintN("     counts as one. Zero means none was seen, not that none happened.")
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwLinkAddressBound - a link of this kind has just been handed an
;  address by something that is not this board's own bring-up.
;
;  TODAY THAT IS THE `dhcp` COMMAND, and it is the whole of a defect that
;  cost a day. That command is portable: it runs DISCOVER/OFFER/REQUEST/
;  ACK over whichever link the policy layer chose and gives the lease to
;  the one IP layer. Over the WIRED link that is the end of the story -
;  the caller writes gEthIp/gEthMask/gEthGw itself and nothing else on
;  this board cares. Over the RADIO it is half the story: wifi.pi4 keeps
;  its own gWifiHaveIp, everything the radio does tests it, and the
;  wireless console will not arm while it says no.
;
;  THE SYMPTOM THAT MADE IT URGENT, 2026-09-06: the board took its
;  address this way, ANSWERED PING - the prompt's TCP tick pumps the IP
;  layer for whichever link is selected, console or no console - and its
;  UDP console on port 5555 was DEAF. With the serial cable out that is a
;  board on the network that nobody can reach, and the only cure was to
;  type `wifi join` on the console that was not working.
;
;  WifiAdoptAddress is idempotent and so is this: a renewal of an address
;  the radio already has re-records the same three numbers and finds the
;  console already listening.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwLinkAddressBound HAS MOVED, and it is BELOW HwLinkOpen now.
;
;  It has to be: since 2026-09-07 it CALLS HwLinkOpen, and this language
;  resolves a call to a procedure it has already seen. See the procedure
;  itself, after HwLinkOpen, for what it does with it and why.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwLinkOpen - the seam's bring-up. Choose an interface, make sure the
;  IP layer's row for it holds that interface's identity, and say in a
;  full sentence why not if nothing can carry traffic. 1 if something
;  now can.
;
;  IT WAS CALLED LinkBringUp UNTIL 2026-09-05 and it lived here under a
;  portable-looking name, which is why Anvil/Core/net_cmd.pbi and
;  Anvil/Core/tcp_cmd.pbi called into a Pi 4 board file four times over
;  without it being obvious that they did. The name now says which side
;  of the seam it is on; Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi states the contract.
;
;  IT IS SILENT ON SUCCESS. It used to print through EthUp / EthUpIp,
;  which wrapped it. The command layer prints which link won and why,
;  because that wording is the same on every board, and asks this board
;  for the rest through HwLinkSayDetail.
;
;  THE ADDRESSES ARE RE-READ BEFORE THE BRING-UP, not after, and that
;  order is the whole point: they live in the settings store, which the
;  operator can change between one transfer and the next - `net server`
;  is exactly the thing somebody retypes after a get has failed - and a
;  port that ignored what was just typed would be the silent wrong answer
;  this project exists to refuse. EthHasIpConfig() below reads them.
;
;  IDEMPOTENT AND CHEAP THE SECOND TIME (eth_HwUp returns 2): once the
;  port is up it stays up, so a run of get commands pays the bring-up
;  once. The re-check is live - GenetStarted() and one MDIO read - so a
;  cable pulled between two commands is noticed rather than lied about.
;
;  needIp = 1  the caller must be able to SEND from an address (ping,
;              dns, get, put, tcp, http get)
;  needIp = 0  the caller has no address yet and is about to ask for one
;              (dhcp), so an interface with a link but no address is a
;              perfectly good answer
;
;  THE MAC AND THE IP MUST COME FROM THE SAME INTERFACE. That is the one
;  thing this procedure exists to make impossible to get wrong: an
;  interface's six bytes and its address are two fields of ONE ROW in
;  net.pi4, keyed by #HW_LINK_*, and writing the wired card's hardware
;  address into the row the radio then builds its frames from produces a
;  board whose every reply is filtered out by its own access point, with
;  nothing anywhere reporting a fault. The per-interface rows make the
;  two halves impossible to CROSS between interfaces; they do not make it
;  impossible to write one row badly, which is why the kind below is
;  spelled out beside each pair rather than carried in a variable.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwLinkOpen(needIp.i)
  Define wiredLink.i
  Define wiredIp.i
  Define wifiUp.i
  Define wifiIp.i
  Define k.i
  Define r.i

  ; ---- HAND THE HwLink BACKEND THE WIRED CARD'S ADDRESS. gEthMac lives
  ; ---- in eth_globals.pi4, which the board file includes long after
  ; ---- hw_link.pi4 has to be included, so the backend keeps a pointer
  ; ---- rather than a second copy of the six bytes. Set here because
  ; ---- this is the one procedure in the monitor that chooses a link,
  ; ---- so nothing can select an interface without it having been set.
  HwLinkSetWiredMacPtr(@gEthMac[0])

  ; ---- what the radio can do. Nothing is started here: the radio is
  ; ---- brought up by `wifi join` or at boot, and a network command is
  ; ---- not the place to start joining one.
  wifiUp = 0
  wifiIp = 0
  If gWifiUp <> 0
    wifiUp = 1
    If gWifiHaveIp <> 0
      wifiIp = 1
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ; ---- what the wired side can do. EthConfigIp() reads the settings
  ; ---- store and validates the three addresses; it is quiet when they
  ; ---- are simply absent, which is the ordinary case on a bench that
  ; ---- runs on Wi-Fi.
  wiredIp = 0
  If EthHasIpConfig() <> 0
    wiredIp = 1
  EndIf

  wiredLink = 0
  If gEthUp = 1
    If GenetStarted() = 1
      If GenetPhyLinkUp() = 1
        wiredLink = 1                  ; already up: one MDIO read
      Else
        gEthUp = 0                     ; the cable has gone since last time
      EndIf
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ; The five-second bring-up is paid only when it is worth paying: the
  ; operator asked for wired, or the radio has nothing.
  If wiredLink = 0
    If LinkPin() = #HW_LINK_WIRED Or wifiIp = 0
      If LinkPin() <> #HW_LINK_WIFI
        If eth_HwUpLocal() > #ETH_HWUP_DOWN
          wiredLink = 1
        EndIf
      EndIf
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ; The operator's override, HANDED TO THE POLICY AS A FACT. It used to
  ; be applied here by zeroing the other interface's two facts before
  ; calling, which is how `net` came to say "there is nothing plugged
  ; into the Ethernet socket" about a cable that was in, negotiated and
  ; holding two addresses: LinkSelect stamped the only reason it had for
  ; choosing the radio with the wired facts clear. Every fact it is
  ; given below is now true, the pin included, and the reason it stamps
  ; describes the instruction that was actually given. The pin is a
  ; #HW_LINK_* kind and lives in RaspberryPi4/Lib/link.pi4 beside the
  ; preference order it overrides.
  k = LinkSelect(LinkPin(), wiredLink, wiredIp, wifiUp, wifiIp)
  If k = #HW_LINK_NONE
    PrintN("!! no interface is carrying traffic, so nothing was done.")
    Print("   ")
    UartWriteStr(LinkWhyText())
    PrintNl()
    PrintN("   Plug a cable into the Ethernet socket and set an address with")
    PrintN("   net ip, or join a wireless network with wifi join, then try again.")
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; ---- fill in the IP layer's row for the interface that won --------
  ; ---- Only that one row: the other interface keeps whatever it was
  ; ---- given, so a `get` over the cable no longer costs the radio the
  ; ---- address its console is answering on.
  If k = #HW_LINK_WIRED
    If NetSetMac(#HW_LINK_WIRED, @gEthMac[0]) = 0
      PrintN("!! the network stack refused this board's wired hardware address, so")
      PrintN("   nothing was done.")
      EthWhyNet()
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    If wiredIp <> 0
      If NetSetIPv4(#HW_LINK_WIRED, gEthIp, gEthMask, gEthGw) = 0
        PrintN("!! the network stack refused the wired addresses, so nothing was")
        PrintN("   done.")
        EthWhyNet()
        ProcedureReturn 0
      EndIf
    ElseIf needIp <> 0
      PrintN("!! the wired link is up but no address is set for it, so nothing")
      PrintN("   could be sent. Type dhcp to be given one, or net ip <a.b.c.d>.")
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  Else
    If NetSetMac(#HW_LINK_WIFI, @wifi_ourMac[0]) = 0
      PrintN("!! the network stack refused the radio's hardware address, so")
      PrintN("   nothing was done.")
      EthWhyNet()
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    If wifiIp <> 0
      If NetSetIPv4(#HW_LINK_WIFI, gWifiIp, gWifiMask, gWifiGw) = 0
        PrintN("!! the network stack refused the radio's addresses, so nothing was")
        PrintN("   done.")
        EthWhyNet()
        ProcedureReturn 0
      EndIf
    ElseIf needIp <> 0
      PrintN("!! the radio is joined but has no address, so nothing could be sent.")
      PrintN("   Type dhcp, or wifi join again.")
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure


; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwLinkAddressBound - a link of this kind has just been handed an
;  address by something that is not this board's own bring-up.
;
;  IT IS BELOW HwLinkOpen BECAUSE IT CALLS IT. That is the 2026-09-07
;  addition and the whole of the second half of this comment.
;
;  TODAY THAT IS THE `dhcp` COMMAND, and it is the whole of a defect that
;  cost a day. That command is portable: it runs DISCOVER/OFFER/REQUEST/
;  ACK over whichever link the policy layer chose and gives the lease to
;  the one IP layer. Over the WIRED link that is the end of the story -
;  the caller writes gEthIp/gEthMask/gEthGw itself and nothing else on
;  this board cares. Over the RADIO it is half the story: wifi.pi4 keeps
;  its own gWifiHaveIp, everything the radio does tests it, and the
;  wireless console will not arm while it says no.
;
;  THE SYMPTOM THAT MADE IT URGENT, 2026-09-06: the board took its
;  address this way, ANSWERED PING - the prompt's TCP tick pumps the IP
;  layer for whichever link is selected, console or no console - and its
;  UDP console on port 5555 was DEAF. With the serial cable out that is a
;  board on the network that nobody can reach, and the only cure was to
;  type `wifi join` on the console that was not working.
;
;  ======================================================================
;  AND A RADIO LEASE MUST NOT TAKE THE ONE IP LAYER OFF THE CABLE.
;  ======================================================================
;  MEASURED OVER THE CABLE, 2026-09-07, with a laptop talking to the
;  board on a link-local address: the access point idled the board out,
;  WifiLinkTick noticed, re-associated and re-leased - and the board
;  printed that Wi-Fi had reconnected with its new lease. That lease went into the
;  ONE IP layer, the console re-armed on the radio, and the wired console
;  the laptop was in the middle of using went silent. The laptop is not
;  on 192.168.1.x and cannot be, so from the bench the board simply
;  vanished, mid-session, with no cable touched and nothing typed.
;
;  IT IS THE INTERFACE PREFERENCE ORDER BEING OVERRULED BY A SIDE
;  EFFECT. LinkSelect's rule is that the wired side wins when both are
;  usable, and RaspberryPi4/Board/boot.pi4 says in as many words that a
;  wireless join must "add a second interface without moving the console
;  off the cable". Nothing enforced it: the lease path applies its
;  result to the shared stack and nobody asks the policy layer whether
;  that link is the one that should be carrying traffic.
;
;  SO THE POLICY LAYER IS ASKED, HERE, ONCE. HwLinkOpen re-derives the
;  whole preference order from live facts - the cable, the PHY's link
;  bit, both addresses, the operator's pin - and reconfigures the IP
;  layer for whichever link wins. On a board with a cable holding an
;  address that puts the stack straight back on the cable, with the
;  radio's own state (gWifiIp, gWifiHaveIp) correctly recorded by the
;  WifiAdoptAddress above; on a board with no cable it selects the radio
;  and the effect is exactly what it was before this line existed.
;
;  IT COSTS ONE MDIO READ ON A LINK THAT IS ALREADY UP, and it cannot
;  pay the five-second bring-up while a radio has an address: that is
;  HwLinkOpen's own rule, unchanged, and the radio has one by
;  construction here. NetAddressBound calls NetConsoleRearm immediately
;  after this returns, so the console is re-derived from the restored
;  state rather than from the moment in the middle of it.
;
;  WifiAdoptAddress is idempotent and so is this: a renewal of an address
;  the radio already has re-records the same three numbers, re-derives
;  the same winner and finds the console already listening.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwLinkAddressBound(kind.i, ip.i, mask.i, gw.i)
  If kind = #HW_LINK_WIFI
    WifiAdoptAddress(ip, mask, gw)
    If ip = 0
      ProcedureReturn
    EndIf
    ; Ask the preference order who should be carrying traffic now. Its
    ; refusals print in full sentences and none of them can happen here -
    ; the radio has just taken an address, so there is always at least
    ; one usable interface - but the answer is not ignored either: if it
    ; somehow could not choose, the stack keeps the radio's lease, which
    ; is the state this procedure was left in before today.
    HwLinkOpen(1)
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  ; The wired side has nothing to do. gEthIp/gEthMask/gEthGw belong to
  ; the caller, which has already written them, and no wired-only feature
  ; on this board waits on an address.
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  WHAT LEFT THIS FILE ON 2026-09-05, AND WHERE IT WENT
; ======================================================================
;  Every one of these was called from Anvil/Core/net_cmd.pbi or
;  Anvil/Core/tcp_cmd.pbi, which is a shared core that is supposed to
;  name no board. None of them touched genet or cyw43; they were here
;  because this is where the network commands' caller half grew up, and
;  that is a history rather than a reason.
;
;    LinkBringUp  -> HwLinkOpen, above. The seam's name for the same act.
;    LinkSay      -> Anvil/Core/net_cmd.pbi. It prints, in words that are
;    PutLinkMac      the same on every board, what the portable policy
;                    layer already knows.
;    LinkMacPtr   -> RaspberryPi4/Lib/link.pi4. It is HwLinkMacPtr of the
;                    kind that won.
;    EthUp        -> Anvil/Core/net_cmd.pbi as NetLinkUpFull /
;    EthUpIp         NetLinkUpSay / HwLinkOpen(0). The three differed only
;    EthLinkUp       in how much configuration went on top and in how much
;                    they printed, and none of that is a property of a MAC.
;    EthResolve   -> Anvil/Core/net_cmd.pbi as NetResolveHop. It is
;                    net.pi4's ARP plus this project's pump plus a
;                    keypress check - portable in every line.
;    EthSendStaged-> Anvil/Core/net_cmd.pbi as NetXferSend.
;    EthPump      -> Anvil/Core/net_cmd.pbi as NetXferPump / NetXferRun.
;    EthRun          tftp.pi4 and net.pi4 are portable libraries and the
;                    loop over them never was anything else.
;    eth_SayUp    -> split: the speed, the duplex, the hardware address
;                    and the four addresses are printed by the shared
;                    command out of the HwLink* getters; the receive
;                    region and the made-up-address warning stayed here
;                    as HwLinkSayDetail, because they are true of this
;                    board and of nothing else.
;
;  WHAT STAYED IS WHAT TOUCHES THE PART: eth_HwUp (probe, MAC, receive
;  region, GenetStart and its four refusals), EthDown/HwLinkClose
;  (GenetStop), and HwLinkOpen's fact-gathering, which reads gEthUp,
;  GenetStarted and one MDIO read. That is the whole of the board's half
;  and it is the half that could never have moved.
; ======================================================================
